## Introduction
An *n-gram model* analyzes training data to determine how frequently specific word sequences occur. For example, given the phrase “my house is,” the model examines all words that follow “my” in the dataset. If “house” is the most common continuation, the model predicts “house.”

In this Post, I will cover the mathematical basics of modern LMs and train my own N-Gram Model based on my lecture notes. 
I'll go into markov chains.


## Conceptually
> This section is largely based on @NLP2026

Most language models use context consisting of $n-1$ tokens $C:= w_1w_2\cdots w_{n-1}$ to predict the *nth* token $w_n$ and describe the probability for every
next token with $P(w_n|w_{1:n-1}) = P(w_n|C)$. N-Gram models limit this context
by assuming $\approx P(w_n| w_{n-N+1} \cdots w_{n-1})$. In the extreme case of a *bigram model*
($N=2$) this assumption simplifies to $P(w_n|C)\approx P(w_n|w_{n-1})$, we call
this the *markov assumption*. The probability of outputting a sentence $s=w_{1}w_{2}\dots w_{m}$ with token-length $m$ based on context token $c$ is $P(s)=\prod_{w\in s}P(w)$. I define $S_{N}$ as the set of all sentences with length N. And $s_{w_n}$
We define $T(a | B)$ as the number of occurrences of $a$ with the sentence $B$ preceeding. The next token selection is decided by $Sel: C \longrightarrow w_n$.

In our case, we define $P(w_{n}|C)=\frac{ T(w_{n}| w_{n-N+1 \cdots w_{n-1}})}{ T(w_{n})}$, simplifying to $P(w_{n}|C) = \frac{T(w_n|w_{n-1})}{T(w_n)}$ with bigrams.

### Bigram ≈ Markov
Let's take the *maximum likelihood assumption* of picking with $Sel(C) := \max_{w_{i}} (P(w_{i}|C))$. This leads to the key insight that a *bigram model* with this $Sel$ is equivalent to a markov chain, where nodes are tokens and edge weights are $P(w_n|w_{n-1})$. 

We can also model higher-order N-Grams as markov chains, where the number of nodes increases exponentially with $N-1$.


<!-- Since we know that $\sum_wP(w|C)=1$, we can choose tokens easily by selecting based on $P$.  -->
### Greedy decoding always ends in a loop
Before we improve $Sel$, let's prove *why* choosing greedily will fail.
#### Proof
We define a greedy $Sel$ as:
$$f: V \longrightarrow V, \qquad f(w) := \operatorname*{arg\,max}_{w' \in V} P(w' \mid w),$$
Given vocabulary $V$ with $|V| = v < \infty$:
Generation from a seed $w_{0}$ is then nothing but iterating:
$$w_{t} = f(w_{t-1}) = f^{t}(w_{0}).$$

> **Theorem.** For every $w_{0} \in V$ the greedy sequence $(w_{t})_{t \ge 0}$ is
> *eventually periodic*: there are $i,j \in \mathbb{R}$ where $i < j \le v$ such that $w_{i} = w_{j}$, and the sequence repeats with an arbitrary period $p$: $f^{p+1}(w_{i})=w_j$

**Proof.** Look at the first $v+1$ tokens $w_{0}, w_{1}, \dots, w_{v}$. They are $v+1$
elements drawn from a set of only $v$ tokens, so by the pigeonhole principle two of them must
coincide: $w_{i} = w_{j}$ for some $0 \le i < j \le v$. Because $f$ is a *function*:

$$w_{i+k} = f^{k}(w_{i}) = f^{k}(w_{j}) = w_{j+k} \quad \text{for all } k \ge 0.$$

So the sequence is periodic with period $p = j - i$ from index $i$ on. $\blacksquare$

#### What do we learn from that?
The generated text therefore has the shape of a $\rho$: a *tail* of at most $v$ tokens,
followed by a *cycle* the model can never escape. Three consequences matter for us:

1. **Repetition is structural, not a bug of the dataset.** The infamous "the the the the" of a
   greedily decoded LLM is the same phenomenon: a deterministic map on a finite state space
   has no other option.
2. **The reachable language is tiny.** Greedy output is fully determined by its seed, so the
   model can produce at most $|V|$ distinct texts — a finite set, out of infinitely many
   grammatical sentences. This is exactly the "locked on a subset" problem of the next
   section, and note that *no amount of training data fixes it*, because the argument never
   touched the counts $T$.
3. **Termination is luck.** A sentence only ends if the tail happens to hit the absorbing stop
   token $\langle/s\rangle$ before the cycle closes; otherwise generation runs forever.

The bound is sharp in the order of the model rather than in the vocabulary: for a general
$N$-gram the state is the whole context $(w_{n-N+1}, \dots, w_{n-1}) \in V^{N-1}$, so the same
proof gives cycles of length at most $|V|^{N-1}$. Larger $N$ buys a longer leash, not freedom.

To prevent this, we sample from $P(\cdot|C)$. Thus, $f$ is no longer a *function*, but a *stochastic kernel* of a markov chain.

### The better selection Function
The maximum likelihood assumption fails in the real world. No matter how big the training set, some sentences will never be able to be generated. That leaves the model locked on a subset $\subset$ of real language.

We use Laplace Smoothing:

$$P_\text{Laplace}(w_{i})=\frac{ T(w_{i} + 1}{ T(w_{n})}$$ **Laplace smoothing**: Just adding 1 for every token


